%[text] # Image-Based Defect Detection for Manufacturing Inspection
%[text] In this project, you will use MATLAB to create a virtual inspection station that processes images of a single part type, produces defect evidence overlays and measurable features, and classifies parts using a pretrained network via transfer learning. You will then evaluate performance and robustness across a repeatable test suite.
%[text] Use this Live Script as a template to get started on your project solution. You may include helper functions at the bottom of this document or as separate files. For a tutorial on how to use Live Scripts, check out this [video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hUXTyPYRidM).
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_5bd4] **Suggested Tasks**
%[text] 1. Explore and Organize the Image Dataset
%[text] 2. Build a Single-Image Inspection Function
%[text] 3. Train and Integrate an AI Classifier into your Single-Image Inspection Function
%[text] 4. Evaluate Inspection System Performance
%[text] 5. Test and Evaluate System Robustness \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Break Down the Problem
%[text] In the space below, define the problem statement:
%[text] - We need a system that looks at pictures of bottles and decides if each one is good (PASS) or has a defect (FAIL), without a person having to check every bottle by hand. \
%[text] List the requirements, constraints, and success criteria for the project:
%[text] Requirements:
%[text] - Take an image of a bottle and output PASS or FAIL
%[text] - Show some evidence for why a bottle was flagged.
%[text] - Work reasonably well even if lighting, blur, or camera noise changes a little \
%[text] Constraints:
%[text] - Only one part type used (bottle, from the MVTec AD dataset)
%[text] - Small dataset (292 images total), with far fewer FAIL examples (63) than PASS examples (229)
%[text] - Must run in MATLAB using tools/toolboxes covered in the course \
%[text] Success criteria:
%[text] - High accuracy on a test set of images the model has never seen
%[text] - Low rate of missing real defects (false accepts), since that's the more serious mistake
%[text] - The system's decisions should be explainable, not just a single confidence number \
%[text] What is your proposed solution?
%[text] Build a two-part inspection system:
%[text] 1. Classical image processing: finds the bottle's opening in each image, measures how round and unbroken its shape is, and checks the brightness inside it, then gives a simple rule-based guess (PASS/FAIL) based on those measurements.
%[text] 2. AI classifier: a pretrained neural network (ResNet-18), fine-tuned on our bottle images, gives the main PASS/FAIL decision with a confidence score. \
%[text] Note: The AI's answer is the main decision. The classical method acts as a secondary sanity-check and gives extra detail for traceability.
%[text] List the steps you will need to take to achieve your proposed solution:
%[text] - Organize the dataset: sort images into PASS/FAIL, store them in a table and datastore
%[text] - Build the classical inspection function which preprocess images, isolate the bottle opening, measure its shape and brightness, add a simple rule-based decision
%[text] - Train the AI classifier by fine-tune ResNet-18 on our images, balance the PASS/FAIL imbalance, combine it with the classical method into one function
%[text] - Evaluate performance by running the system on a batch of test images, check accuracy, confusion matrix, yield/defect rates
%[text] - Test robustness: see how well the system holds up if lighting, blur, or noise change \
%[text] What challenges do you face in the process of achieving the solution?
%[text] - Class imbalance: far fewer FAIL images than PASS, so the model could just learn to guess PASS most of the time. We addressed this with a weighted loss during training.
%[text] - Overcautious AI predictions: on the clean, Undistorted test set, the AI classifier rejected the large majority of good bottles, showing a strong bias toward FAIL. This means the model rarely misses a real defect, but at the cost of flagging many good ones.
%[text] - Small dataset: only 63 FAIL images total makes results sensitive to which images happen to land in the test set, so we're being careful not to over-trust any single split of results. \
%%
%[text] ## Task 1: Explore and Organize Image Dataset 
%[text] Your goal in this step is to define defect classes and labels (keep it small).
%[text] - Load and inspect the images and labels from your chosen dataset (see the project page on GitHub for recommended datasets) to understand the part types, defect classes, and data balance
%[text] - Store labels in a table loaded by `readtable(...)`
%[text] - Manage images and labels with `imageDatastore(...)` and the `Labels` property (or use the example workflow for setting up labeled image datastores).
%[text] - Helpful reference: [<u>Create and Explore Datastore for Image Classification</u>](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/ug/create-and-explore-datastore-for-image-classification.html).
%[text] - Choose 2-3 defect types (for FAIL) plus PASS \
