%[text] ## Task 2: Build a Single-Image Inspection Function
%[text] Your goal in this step is to create a function that analyzes and image to identify and quantify suspected defects detected in the image. You're trying to answer the question: "What in the image looks suspicious?"
%[text] To build this function you will need to:
%[text] **STEP 1. Standardize the images**
%[text] - Read images with `imread(...)` or through `imageDatastore(...)`.
%[text] - Standardize size with `imresize(...)` so metrics and model input are consistent.
%[text] - Convert to grayscale with `rgb2gray(...)` as needed (e.g. classical processing may rely on intensity values) \
%[text] **STEP 2. Preprocess the images**
%[text] - Apply classical preprocessing, prioritizing stability over complexity. In other words, apply only what you need to reduce sensitivity to normal station variation. Suggested processing includes:
%[text] - Lighting correction: `imflatfield(...)` (or background estimate with `imgaussfilt(...)` and subtraction)
%[text] - Contrast normalization: `adapthisteq(...)`
%[text] - Denoising: `medfilt2(...)` or `imgaussfilt(...)`
%[text] - Save intermediate results during development (e.g., `imshowpair(...)`) for quick debugging. \
%[text] **STEP 3.** ***Optionally*****, define a region of interest (ROI)**
%[text] If each image already contains a centered part with minimal clutter, skip ROI and use the full image:
%[text] - `roi = I;` \
%[text] If needed, define ROI fully automatically (no per-image manual selection), by using, for example:
%[text] - Fixed crop defined once (camera is consistent): indexing or `imcrop(...)` OR
%[text] - Auto-crop from the part mask: segment the part, compute `BoundingBox` using `regionprops(...)`, then `imcrop(...)`. \
%[text] **STEP 4. Segment the image for an evidence overlay**
%[text] Your goal is to generate an evidence overlay and a few measurable numbers—not a perfect segmentation. Generate a simple binary mask that highlights suspicious regions in the image. Use a defect-appropriate recipe and keep it short:
%[text] - Start with thresholding: `imbinarize(...)` or `adaptthresh(...)` + `imbinarize(...)`
%[text] - Clean small specks: `bwareaopen(...)`
%[text] - Bridge gaps (if appropriate): `imclose(...)`
%[text] - Fill holes (only when it matches your goal (e.g., for a clean outer part silhouette): `imfill(...,'holes')` \
%[text] Deliverable: `maskEvidence = defectEvidence(roi)`
%[text] **STEP 5. Extract a small set of interpretable metrics**
%[text] Your goal is to quantify the evidence for suspected defects, which supports traceability and debugging in your workflow. These metrics should answer the question: “What did the algorithm see, and how strong was the evidence?”
%[text] These measurements are mainly for traceability (inspection logs), debugging, and sanity checks. Keep it to 3–4 metrics so it stays easy.
%[text] Recommended minimal metrics (works for many defect types):
%[text] - `numComponents`: how many separate suspicious regions were found (use `bwconncomp(maskEvidence)` and read `NumObjects`)
%[text] - `maxArea`: size of the largest suspicious region (use `regionprops(maskEvidence,'Area')`)
%[text] - `areaRatio`: fraction of the ROI flagged as suspicious (`nnz(maskEvidence) / numel(maskEvidence)`)
%[text] - *(optional)* `edgeDensity`: if your evidence comes from edges (chips/scratches), measure how “edgy” the ROI is (`nnz(edgeMask) / numel(edgeMask)`) \
%[text] **STEP 6.** ***Optionally*****, implement a rule-based baseline.**
%[text] Implement 2–3 conservative rules so your system can always produce an explainable fallback decision mechanism. This can also be a useful comparison with AI result.
%[text] - Example: fail if `maxArea > Amax` or `numComponents > Nmax` \
%[text] Deliverable: `baselineDecision = decideRules(evidence)`
%[output:36520d98] %[output:7db2994e] %[output:9248315a] %[output:4db378f0] %[output:8d6392bf] %[output:152c13fe]


%%
%[text] ## Task 3: Train and Integrate an AI Classifier into your Single-Image Inspection Function
%[text] Your goal in this step is to use a MathWorks provided pretrained network (recommended: `resnet18`) to classify each part as either a PASS or FAIL, that will be part of the output of the function you began to build in the previous step.
%[text] - Load the recommended starter network: use `imagePretrainedNetwork` and select `resnet18` from the Deep Learning Toolbox.
%[text] - Resize/augment input with `augmentedImageDatastore(...)` to match the network input size.
%[text] - Fine-tune the last layers for your classes (start with `PASS` vs `FAIL`), using `trainNetwork(...)` or a guided workflow such as [Get started with transfer learning](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/gs/get-started-with-transfer-learning.html) or [pretrained network workflows](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/ug/pretrained-convolutional-neural-networks.html).
%[text] - The output of your AI classification model should be a predicted label (PASS/FAIL) and a confidence score in that label \
%[text] Deliverable: `[aiLabel, aiScore] = classify(net, roiForNet)` 
%[output:8d315456] %[output:8118f285] %[output:01336903] %[output:834b266b] %[output:4fff1bf8] %[output:7f2b9133] %[output:979f8cba] %[output:0fa3667b] %[output:4a819a8b] %[output:47ab2bb6] %[output:18f86e68]
%%
%[text] ## Task 3 Checkpoint: Combine outputs into a hybrid inspection result
%[text] Your image inspection function (e.g. `inspectPar(I)`) should combine the classical evidence with the AI classification output. 
%[text] In other words, your image inspection function should integrate both classical evidence from image processing and an AI classification decision. The AI classifier provides the main decision (PASS/FAIL) while the classical evidence extraction provides interpretability, traceability, and sanity checks for that decision.
%[text] Your function should return:
%[text] - `finalLabel` (from AI, in Task 3)
%[text] - `confidenceScore` (from AI, in Task 3)
%[text] - `evidenceOverlay` (from Step 4 in Task 2)
%[text] - `evidenceMetrics` (from Step 5 in Task 2)
%[text] - (optional) `baselineDecision` and a “disagreement flag” if optional rules in Step 6 from Task 2 and AI output disagree \
%[text] Optionally, you can also choose to overlay these results on the image using `insertShape(...)` / `insertText(...)`, and save rejects with `imwrite(...)`

%%
%[text] ## Task 4: Evaluate Inspection System Performance
%[text] Now that you've built a hybrid inspection system that integrates classical evidence with an AI decision, your goal in this step is to test the system on a batch of images.
%[text] The results of this test should be summarized with a confusion matrix, report yield, and defect rates across the entire batch of images.
%[text] Write a script (e.g. `runInspectionSuite.m`) that:
%[text] - Splits data into train/test (e.g., `splitEachLabel(...)` or a fixed split)
%[text] - Evaluates the trained AI model and trains the inspection system on a test set and logs outcomes
%[text] - Produces a confusion matrix with `confusionmat(...)` / `confusionchart(...)`
%[text] - Summarizes yield and defect counts in tables and plots
%[text] - Visualizes common failure cases (e.g. by creating a montage of common errors using `montage(...)`) \
%[output:6694da86] %[output:4c12e0cc] %[output:0308ff14] %[output:6d2ff2f6] %[output:8092abdd] %[output:298b9717] %[output:90610bab] %[output:1528e66b] %[output:5789c093] %[output:40b4a836] %[output:7339c3bd] %[output:40d78a61] %[output:4698bae4] %[output:320398d4] %[output:90a0f94a] %[output:77db3f84] %[output:4f2bf99f] %[output:959cb564]

