%[text] ## Task 5: Test and Evaluate System Robustness
%[text] Your goal in this step is to assess how your system performs under simulated variations. You can simulate image inspection station variation by altering the lighting, blur, or noise in the images. Here are some suggested distortions to apply:
%[text] - Brightness/contrast: `imadjust(...)`
%[text] - Blur: `imgaussfilt(...)`
%[text] - Noise: `imnoise(...)` \
%[text] Then, re-run the evaluation. In other words, run `runInspectionSuite.m` again with the same image set after applying distortions to the images.
%[text] Compare how performance changes across conditions by reporting how accuracy and false-reject rates change under simulated variations.
%[output:60f1f95f] %[output:94fd584b] %[output:5a14d4f6] %[output:4430e3bd] %[output:0c941572] %[output:72388bdc] %[output:2a5eba5c] %[output:6de92ac6] %[output:73bbb2d9]

%%
%[text] ## Interpretation of Results
%[text] Summarize your findings by interpreting their physical/engineering meaning:
%[text] - Our system combines a rule-based classical check with an AI classifier trained on ResNet-18. The classical check looks at the bottle opening itself: how round and unbroken its shape is, and how bright or dark the middle of the opening looks. The AI classifier makes the actual PASS/FAIL call.
%[text] - Our robustness results show the system performs well and consistently across most simulated conditions. Baseline, brightness, and blur all land in the 90-95% accuracy range, with false reject rates staying low (around 0-2%), meaning the system rarely rejects a good bottle by mistake under these conditions.
%[text] - Noise is the one condition where performance clearly weakens. Accuracy drops somewhat, and the false reject rate rises to around 10%, meaning sensor noise pushes the system toward rejecting more good bottles than it should. Importantly, this is a "safe" failure direction: the system becomes overly cautious rather than letting defects slip through. \
%[text] What are some limitations of your work?
%[text] - Sensor noise remains the system's weakest point, with a noticeably higher false reject rate than any other tested condition.
%[text] - Small FAIL sample size (63 total, 13 in the test set) means any single accuracy number is somewhat sensitive to which images land in the test split.
%[text] - Single part type and camera angle, results are specific to this bottle-neck dataset and may not transfer directly to other parts or camera setups. \
%[text] What are practical next steps?
%[text] - Investigate why noise specifically raises the false reject rate, likely by adding noise-augmented training data so the model learns to stay confident under noisy conditions.
%[text] - Revisit the class-weighting scheme. Since the classifier is heavily biased toward FAIL even on clean images, the next step is testing different weight ratios (or resampling the training set) to find a better balance between catching real defects and not over-flagging good bottles. \
%[text] 

